                <pb facs="0391" n="359" xml:id="pg359"/>

                <head>116. Letter From Prime Minister Eden to President Eisenhower<anchor n="1" xml:id="d116-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                            calendar="gregorian" notAfter="1956-05-02T23:59:59-04:00"
                            notBefore="1956-05-01T00:00:00-04:00"
                    >undated</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Friend</hi>: Thank you for your letter of April
                    27 about trade controls. I must frankly tell you that your suggestion of adding
                    to the embargoed list presents most serious difficulties for us. I do not see
                    how we could agree to this now.</p>
                <p>As to the Chincom list, we greatly need
                    appreciable relaxation, particularly because of progress toward self-government
                    in our colonial territories. A country like Malaya simply does not understand
                    why it has to accept restrictions which are not in operation in, for example,
                    Ceylon.</p>
                <p>But Selwyn will talk all this over with
                        Foster in Paris in the next few
                    days.</p>
                <p>Kindest regards,</p>
                <p>Yours ever,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                            >ANTHONY</hi><anchor n="2" xml:id="d116-f2"
                    /></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
